230	
231	<style>
232	  /* MDX content comes from a separately-compiled component tree (rendered via
233	     <Content /> in [slug].astro), so Astro's default *scoped* styles never
234	     attach to it — every rule here needs :global() or it silently does nothing. */
235	  /* Two separate spacing tokens, easy to conflate but serving different
236	     jobs:
237	       --ping-section-gap   — vertical rhythm BETWEEN sections. Stays a
238	                               fixed 60px at every viewport width — this is
239	                               breathing room in the reading flow, not
240	                               something that should compress on mobile.
241	       --ping-card-padding   — the INSET inside a bordered/gradient card
242	                               (Problem/Approach/Solution) and the
243	                               <article> wrapper's own horizontal margin.
244	                               This one DOES scale down on narrow screens
245	                               (24px mobile / 40px tablet / 60px desktop,
246	                               matching <article>'s own
247	                               px-6 sm:px-10 lg:px-[60px]) — a flat 60px
248	                               inset squished the readable column badly on
249	                               phones. Collapsing both into one variable
250	                               previously made the inter-section gap shrink
251	                               along with the card padding, which read as
252	                               sections crowding each other on mobile. */
253	  .article-body {
254	    --ping-section-gap: 3.75rem; /* 60px, fixed at every width */
255	    --ping-card-padding: 1.5rem; /* 24px */
256	  }
257	
258	  @media (min-width: 40rem) {
259	    .article-body {
260	      --ping-card-padding: 2.5rem; /* 40px */
261	    }
262	  }
263	
264	  @media (min-width: 64rem) {
265	    .article-body {
266	      --ping-card-padding: 3.75rem; /* 60px */
267	    }
268	  }
269	
270	  /* Each top-level block in the MDX body is wrapped in its own
271	     `.ping-section`, so this gap can sit on both sides of a section as
272	     real padding (padding needs a real box on each edge — a
273	     margin-collapse-via-adjacency-selector approach only works for a flat
274	     list of siblings, and margin between siblings COLLAPSES rather than
275	     stacking, which reads as inconsistent spacing once some sections also
276	     have their own border/background — a card's edge should have a
277	     definite, non-collapsing gap around it). The first section only gets
278	     bottom padding, matching Figma's Intro frame using pb-[60px] instead
279	     of py-[60px] since there's nothing above it to pad against.
280	     Problem/Approach/Solution below add their own padding-LEFT/RIGHT only
281	     (not the `padding` shorthand) — the shorthand would overwrite this
282	     rule's padding-top/-bottom outright, since both rules have equal
283	     specificity and the later one wins on every side it touches. Setting
284	     only the two horizontal longhands here means their own left/right
285	     inset composes with (rather than replaces) this rule's vertical gap. */
286	  .article-body :global(.ping-section) {
287	    padding-top: var(--ping-section-gap);
288	    padding-bottom: var(--ping-section-gap);
289	  }
290	
291	  /* Only the intro drops its top padding (nothing above it to pad against). A
292	     bare :first-child would also strip the Problem card's top padding, since
293	     that card is the first child of its .ps-card wrapper. */
294	  .article-body :global(#intro) {
295	    padding-top: 0;
296	  }
297	
298	  /* "The problem" gets a plain 1px stroke outline and its own left/right
299	     inset (on top of the padding-top/-bottom every section already has
300	     from the rule above) — the one section styled as a bordered card
301	     rather than a plain full-bleed block. padding-top/-bottom here
302	     re-declare --ping-section-gap (the FIXED token), not
303	     --ping-card-padding (the responsive one) — using the responsive
304	     variable for the vertical sides was the exact bug this replaced:
305	     it made this section's vertical gap shrink along with its horizontal
306	     inset on mobile, instead of staying fixed like every other section's. */
307	  .article-body :global(.ping-section--problem) {
308	    border: 1px solid var(--section-border-color);
309	    border-radius: 0.75rem; /* 12px */
310	    padding-top: var(--ping-section-gap);
311	    padding-bottom: var(--ping-section-gap);
312	    padding-left: var(--ping-card-padding);
313	    padding-right: var(--ping-card-padding);
314	    margin-top: var(--ping-card-padding);
315	    margin-bottom: var(--ping-card-padding);
316	  }
317	
318	  /* "The approach" gets the same left/right inset, matching "The problem"
319	     above — vertical padding stays on the fixed --ping-section-gap for
320	     the same reason. position: relative gives the ::after grid layer
321	     below a containing block to size itself against. Unlike "The problem",
322	     this section stays borderless/full-bleed on purpose — the border is
323	     what marks Problem as the one "card" section, and adding one here
324	     would blur that distinction. No overflow: hidden here (unlike the
325	     original version of this rule) — the ::after grid layer below is
326	     deliberately wider than this section's own box (it bleeds 40px past
327	     the left/right edges), and clipping at the section boundary would
328	     hide exactly that bleed. The page-level overflow-x-hidden on <body>
329	     (BaseLayout.astro) is what stops that bleed from ever causing a
330	     horizontal scrollbar on narrow viewports. */
331	  .article-body :global(.ping-section--approach) {
332	    position: relative;
333	    padding-top: var(--ping-section-gap);
334	    padding-bottom: var(--ping-section-gap);
335	    padding-left: var(--ping-card-padding);
336	    padding-right: var(--ping-card-padding);
337	  }
338	
339	  /* Decorative dot-grid background for "The approach". A single tiled
340	     radial-gradient draws a 1px dot every 24px using the same weak
341	     --section-border-color hairline already used for "The problem"'s
342	     outline, so it reads as part of this design system rather than a
343	     borrowed effect. (An earlier version of this used a two-axis
344	     repeating-linear-gradient line grid, matching aphera.co's reference;
345	     swapped to dots after finding a closer reference. A per-project
346	     accent-color tint was also tried and reverted — read as too loud/
347	     colorful for this background texture even at 25% strength; the
348	     neutral hairline color works better.)
349	     Deliberately ::after, not ::before: generated content always paints
350	     after an element's real children by spec, so ::after guarantees this
351	     stays behind the section's images/copy without depending on z-index
352	     stacking order against the LightboxImage markup (which has its own
353	     position: relative wrapper). The negative z-index below is
354	     belt-and-suspenders on top of that. Also inert to pointer/click
355	     interaction. Starts fully transparent — observeGridReveal()
356	     (src/lib/animations.ts) adds .grid-active via IntersectionObserver
357	     once the section scrolls into view, which is what actually plays the
358	     gridSequence fade (defined in global.css). */
359	  .article-body :global(.ping-section--approach::after) {
360	    content: "";
361	    position: absolute;
362	    /* Bleeds the grid 40px past the section's own left/right edges (top/bottom
363	       stay flush) so it reads as wider than the text column above it, similar
364	       to aphera.co's reference. This section has no overflow: hidden of its
365	       own (unlike "The problem"/"The solution") specifically so this bleed
366	       isn't clipped — the page-level overflow-x-hidden on <body>
367	       (BaseLayout.astro) is what stops it from ever causing a horizontal
368	       scrollbar on narrow viewports instead. */
369	    inset: 0 -40px;
370	    /* "1px, transparent 1px" (not "0, transparent 1px") is what makes a
371	       crisp dot instead of a soft blurred blob — the color stays solid from
372	       center out to the 1px radius, then cuts hard to transparent at that
373	       same radius. */
374	    background-image: radial-gradient(
375	      circle,
376	      var(--section-border-color) 1px,
377	      transparent 1px
378	    );
379	    background-size: 24px 24px;
380	    /* Fades the grid out near all four edges instead of cutting off hard
381	       — two gradients (one per axis) multiplied together via
382	       mask-composite: intersect, so a point only stays fully opaque where
383	       BOTH the horizontal and vertical fade are opaque (i.e. away from
384	       every edge at once). -webkit- variants are Safari's still-required
385	       prefixed equivalents (mask-composite ergo is broadly supported
386	       unprefixed now, but -webkit-mask-composite uses different keyword
387	       values, hence "source-in" there vs "intersect" above). */
388	    mask-image:
389	      linear-gradient(
390	        to right,
391	        transparent 0%,
392	        rgba(0, 0, 0, 1) 10%,
393	        rgba(0, 0, 0, 1) 90%,
394	        transparent 100%
395	      ),
396	      linear-gradient(
397	        to bottom,
398	        transparent 0%,
399	        rgba(0, 0, 0, 1) 10%,
400	        rgba(0, 0, 0, 1) 90%,
401	        transparent 100%
402	      );
403	    mask-composite: intersect;
404	    -webkit-mask-image:
405	      linear-gradient(
406	        to right,
407	        transparent 0%,
408	        rgba(0, 0, 0, 1) 10%,
409	        rgba(0, 0, 0, 1) 90%,
410	        transparent 100%
411	      ),
412	      linear-gradient(
413	        to bottom,
414	        transparent 0%,
415	        rgba(0, 0, 0, 1) 10%,
416	        rgba(0, 0, 0, 1) 90%,
417	        transparent 100%
418	      );
419	    -webkit-mask-composite: source-in;
420	    pointer-events: none;
421	    z-index: -1;
422	    opacity: 0;
423	  }
424	
425	  .article-body :global(.ping-section--approach.grid-active::after) {
426	    animation: gridSequence 1.4s ease-out forwards;
427	  }
428	
429	  /* "The solution" gets a translucent border and a subtle vertical-fade
430	     background, both tinted by the project's own --icon-color (set inline
431	     in the MDX, resolved the same way the headline accent and Postscript's
432	     divider already do) — so each case study gets a matching treatment in
433	     its own accent color instead of every project showing Ping's red.
434	     color-mix() against transparent is what lets a var()-sourced color
435	     carry a percentage opacity — CSS's plain `color / alpha%` syntax only
